<description>
Rotate each vertical bar's color one slot right and its height one slot left.

Input structure
- A dominant background surrounds several monochrome one-cell-wide vertical bars.
- Every bar is bottom-aligned and occupies a distinct column.
- The ordered bar slots are read from left to right.

Rule
- Keep the grid shape and the bar columns unchanged.
- At slot i, use the color from the previous slot cyclically.
- At slot i, use the height from the next slot cyclically.

Key insight
- Color and height are independent cyclic attributes of the ordered bar slots:
  colors rotate right while heights rotate left.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars ordered left-to-right",
  "transform": "redraw each bar in the same column with the previous bar's color cyclically and the next bar's height cyclically"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid:
    shape: square, side = 2*N+1 where N is bar count
    background: 7
  bars:
    count: 3-5
    placement: one per odd column (1, 3, ..., 2N-1), evenly spaced with gap=1 bg column
    alignment: bottom-aligned, 1-cell wide, contiguous
    height: 1 to side-1 per bar; not all bars same height [observed]
    colors: all distinct, each differs from background (7); any non-bg color allowed
  transform:
    columns: unchanged between input and output
    color_rotation: each bar gets the color of the previous bar (cyclic right shift)
    height_rotation: each bar gets the height of the next bar (cyclic left shift)

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (constraints are simple enough for forward generation)
  steps:
    1. Pick N in [3,5]; grid side S = 2*N+1
    2. Sample N distinct non-7 colors from 0-9
    3. Sample N heights from [1, S-1] ensuring not all identical
    4. Place bars at columns 1,3,...,2N-1, each bottom-aligned with its color and height
    5. Fill remaining cells with 7
  output_derivation:
    For bar i, set color = bars[(i-1) % N].color, height = bars[(i+1) % N].height
```
